/* ================================================================ weathering and caves */
{
  id: 'weathering-and-caves', parent: 'what-the-water-has-been-doing', title: 'Weathering and caves', level: 2,
  short: 'Rain picks up carbon dioxide and becomes a weak acid; the acid dissolves limestone into caves and turns feldspar into clay. Every mole of rock that dissolves takes a mole of CO₂ from the air, which is how a wet planet holds its own thermostat.',
  keywords: ['weathering', 'carbonic acid', 'limestone', 'karst', 'cave', 'stalactite', 'stalagmite', 'dissolution', 'hydrolysis', 'feldspar', 'clay', 'Mammoth Cave', 'carbonate–silicate cycle', 'Walker Hays Kasting', 'CO2 thermostat'],
  prereq: ['gases-in-water', 'water-the-amphoteric', 'rivers-carve'],
  related: ['hard-water-and-limescale', 'the-dielectric-constant', 'water-made-the-air', 'the-deep-time-of-water', 'earth-may-always-have-been-wet', 'chemistry:equilibrium', 'chemistry:solubility-product', 'chemistry:henrys-law', 'chemistry:acid-base-theory'],
  body: `
Rain is not pure water, even over a clean ocean. As a drop falls it takes up carbon dioxide from the air, and a small part of it reacts: $\\ce{CO2 + H2O <=> H2CO3}$, **carbonic acid**, a weak acid that gives the drop a pH of about 5.6. That is a little less acid than black coffee, and given a few hundred thousand years it can dissolve a mountain.

### Limestone
Limestone is calcium carbonate, $\\ce{CaCO3}$, the shells of ancient sea life pressed into rock. The acid attacks it: $\\ce{CaCO3 + CO2 + H2O -> Ca^2+ + 2HCO3-}$. Calcium and bicarbonate ions go into solution, and water, whose dielectric constant of 78 keeps the oppositely charged ions apart, carries them away. A litre of water in equilibrium with the open air can hold about 50 mg of limestone; water that has soaked through soil, where roots and microbes raise the CO₂ to a few per cent of the air, holds three to five times more. So the water does its work underground. It enters along joints and bedding planes, widens them, finds one another, and the rock turns into a plumbing system: a **karst**, named for the Kras plateau of Slovenia, with sinkholes, vanishing streams, springs and caves. Mammoth Cave in Kentucky has more than 680 km of mapped passage, and it is not finished.

### The reaction runs backwards, too
When a drop of saturated water reaches a cave, the air there has less CO₂ than the water, so the water gives some up and the reaction reverses: calcite is deposited. The result is a **stalactite** on the ceiling, a stalagmite where the drop lands, a curtain of flowstone. The growth is slow — a tenth of a millimetre a year is typical, so a metre takes 10,000 years — and it is the same chemistry as the limescale in a kettle ([[hard-water-and-limescale]]).

### Granite becomes clay
Silicate minerals dissolve too, much more slowly. Feldspar, the commonest mineral in the crust, goes by **hydrolysis**: $\\ce{2KAlSi3O8 + 2H2CO3 + 9H2O -> Al2Si2O5(OH)4 + 4H4SiO4 + 2K+ + 2HCO3-}$. The feldspar becomes clay (kaolinite), the silicon leaves as dissolved silica, and the potassium and bicarbonate go to the sea. Hard rock falls apart into soil, the rivers carry the ions to the ocean, and the ocean's shell-builders turn them back into limestone.

### A thermostat
Both reactions consume carbon dioxide: one mole of $\\ce{CO2}$ for each mole of calcite, and for each calcium silicate that weathers a net mole is buried in the sea as new limestone. Volcanoes put CO₂ into the air at roughly 0.1 to 0.3 gigatonnes of carbon a year; weathering takes it out. And weathering speeds up when it is warm and wet. Warm the planet and the rocks eat more CO₂, the greenhouse weakens and the planet cools; cool it and the reverse happens. This negative feedback, set out by Walker, Hays and Kasting in 1981, acts over hundreds of thousands of years, and it is why Earth stayed within reach of liquid water while the Sun grew 30 % brighter ([[the-deep-time-of-water]]). On a planet with no liquid water there is nothing to dissolve the rock and the thermostat is missing; that is part of the story of Venus.

> [!key] Rain plus CO₂ is a weak acid that dissolves limestone into caves and turns feldspar into clay, and each mole of rock removed takes a mole of CO₂ from the air. Because weathering is faster when it is warm, the process holds the climate steady.`,
  ideas: ['Rain in clean air has a pH of about 5.6 because it holds dissolved CO₂ as carbonic acid.', 'Calcite dissolves as CaCO₃ + CO₂ + H₂O → Ca²⁺ + 2HCO₃⁻, and water in contact with CO₂-rich soil air carries three to five times as much as water in open air.', 'The reaction reverses in a cave as the drop gives up CO₂: stalactites grow at about a tenth of a millimetre a year.', 'Silicate weathering turns feldspar into clay; both kinds of weathering consume CO₂, and the rate rises with temperature, a thermostat that works over 10⁵ years.'],
  pitfalls: ['"Weathering of limestone releases CO₂" — It takes CO₂ up: the carbon in the dissolved bicarbonate came from the air. (The limestone burns CO₂ out only in a kiln, or when the sea precipitates it again.)', '"Caves are carved by streams eroding the rock" — Most caves are dissolved, not scoured: the water is a chemical, not a mechanical, agent, and the passages grow where slow, slightly acid water meets bare rock.', '"Acid rain is a modern problem" — All rain is slightly acid. Pollution (sulfur and nitrogen oxides) made it stronger, pH 4 or lower; the 5.6 of clean rain comes from the CO₂ in the air.'],
  terms: [
    { term: 'Carbonic acid', also: ['H₂CO₃', 'dissolved CO₂'], def: 'The weak acid formed when CO₂ dissolves in water; in practice most of the dissolved CO₂ stays as CO₂, and only a small part exists as H₂CO₃. Water in equilibrium with today\'s air has a pH of about 5.6.' },
    { term: 'Karst', also: ['karst landscape', 'karst aquifer'], def: 'A landscape formed by the dissolution of soluble rock, mostly limestone: sinkholes, caves, underground streams and springs. About a quarter of the world\'s population drinks karst water.' },
    { term: 'Chemical weathering', also: ['weathering', 'dissolution', 'hydrolysis'], def: 'The breakdown of rock by chemical reaction with water and its dissolved gases: dissolution of carbonates and the hydrolysis of silicates into clay and ions. It consumes CO₂.' },
    { term: 'Speleothem', also: ['stalactite', 'stalagmite', 'flowstone'], def: 'A mineral deposit formed in a cave from dripping or flowing water, almost always calcite: a stalactite hangs from the ceiling, a stalagmite grows up from the floor. Growth: about 0.1 mm a year.' },
    { term: 'Carbonate–silicate cycle', also: ['long-term carbon cycle', 'weathering thermostat', 'Walker feedback'], def: 'The slow loop in which volcanoes release CO₂, silicate weathering draws it down, and the sea buries the product as limestone. Because weathering is faster in a warmer, wetter climate, it stabilises the climate over about 10⁵ years.' }
  ],
  formulas: [
    { name: 'pH of rain from the CO₂ in the air', expr: 'pH = -log(K1*KH*x)/2', tex: '\\mathrm{pH} = -\\tfrac{1}{2}\\log_{10}\\left(K_1\\, K_H\\, x\\right)',
      vars: { pH: { name: 'pH of the water', tex: '\\mathrm{pH}' }, K1: { name: 'first dissociation constant of carbonic acid', q: false, unit: 'mol/L', value: 4.45e-7, fixed: true, tex: 'K_1', hint: 'At 25 °C.' }, KH: { name: 'Henry\'s constant of CO₂', q: false, unit: 'mol/(L·atm)', value: 0.034, fixed: true, tex: 'K_H', hint: 'How much CO₂ dissolves per atmosphere of CO₂ pressure, at 25 °C.' }, x: { name: 'CO₂ in the air', q: 'ratio', unit: 'ppm', value: 420, hint: '420 ppm in the air today; soil air holds 5,000 to 50,000 ppm.' } },
      solveFor: 'pH', note: 'At 1 atm total pressure, neglecting everything but the first dissociation: $[\\ce{H+}] = \\sqrt{K_1 K_H x}$. Clean rain comes out at 5.6.',
      stories: { pH: 'The air holds {x} of CO₂. What is the pH of water in equilibrium with it?', x: 'Water with a pH of {pH} is in equilibrium with air. How much CO₂ does the air hold?' } },
    { name: 'Limestone a litre can dissolve', expr: 'c = 100090*cbrt(KH*K1*Ksp*x/(4*K2))', tex: 'c = 1.0\\times10^{5}\\,\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{K_H\\, K_1\\, K_{sp}\\, x}{4\\, K_2}}',
      vars: { c: { name: 'CaCO₃ dissolved at equilibrium', q: false, unit: 'mg/L' }, KH: { name: 'Henry\'s constant of CO₂', q: false, unit: 'mol/(L·atm)', value: 0.034, fixed: true, tex: 'K_H' }, K1: { name: 'first dissociation constant', q: false, unit: 'mol/L', value: 4.45e-7, fixed: true, tex: 'K_1' }, Ksp: { name: 'solubility product of calcite', q: false, unit: 'mol²/L²', value: 3.3e-9, fixed: true, tex: 'K_{sp}' }, K2: { name: 'second dissociation constant', q: false, unit: 'mol/L', value: 4.69e-11, fixed: true, tex: 'K_2' }, x: { name: 'CO₂ in the air', q: 'ratio', unit: '%', value: 0.042, hint: '0.042 % in open air; 1 to 5 % in soil.' } },
      solveFor: 'c', note: 'An open-system estimate at 25 °C ignoring activity coefficients, which raise real values by a quarter or so. The 1.0 × 10⁵ turns mol/L of CaCO₃ into mg/L.',
      stories: { c: 'Water meets limestone while the air in contact with it holds {x} of CO₂. How much calcium carbonate can a litre carry?', x: 'A litre of water carries {c} of dissolved calcite. What CO₂ content of the air does that imply?' } },
    { name: 'Growth of a stalactite', expr: 'L = r*t', tex: 'L = r\\, t',
      vars: { L: { name: 'length grown', q: false, unit: 'm' }, r: { name: 'growth rate', q: false, unit: 'mm/yr', value: 0.1, hint: 'Typically 0.01 to 1 mm a year; a tenth is common.' }, t: { name: 'time', q: false, unit: 'kyr', value: 10 } },
      solveFor: 'L', note: 'Millimetres a year times thousands of years gives metres directly.',
      stories: { L: 'A stalactite grows at {r}. How long is it after {t}?', t: 'A stalactite {L} long grows at {r}. How old is it?' } },
    { name: 'CO₂ taken up by dissolving limestone', expr: 'mc = ml*Mc/Ml', tex: 'm_{\\mathrm{CO_2}} = m_l\\,\\frac{M_c}{M_l}',
      vars: { mc: { name: 'CO₂ consumed', q: 'mass', unit: 't', tex: 'm_{\\mathrm{CO_2}}' }, ml: { name: 'limestone dissolved', q: 'mass', unit: 't', value: 1000, tex: 'm_l' }, Mc: { name: 'molar mass of CO₂', value: 44.01, fixed: true, tex: 'M_c' }, Ml: { name: 'molar mass of CaCO₃', value: 100.09, fixed: true, tex: 'M_l' } },
      solveFor: 'mc', note: 'One mole of CO₂ for each mole of CaCO₃, from CaCO₃ + CO₂ + H₂O → Ca²⁺ + 2HCO₃⁻.',
      stories: { mc: 'Water dissolves {ml} of pure limestone. How much CO₂ does it take from the air?' } }
  ],
  examples: [
    { title: 'Why soil water eats rock', q: 'Compare the pH of water in equilibrium with the open air (420 ppm CO₂) with water in equilibrium with soil air (3 % CO₂), and the limestone each could carry.',
      steps: [{ text: 'Open air:', tex: '\\mathrm{pH} = -\\tfrac12\\log_{10}(4.45\\times10^{-7}\\times0.034\\times4.2\\times10^{-4}) = 5.60' }, { text: 'Soil air (x = 0.03):', tex: '\\mathrm{pH} = -\\tfrac12\\log_{10}(4.45\\times10^{-7}\\times0.034\\times0.03) = 4.67' }, 'The dissolved limestone scales as the cube root of the CO₂: 71 times as much CO₂ gives $71^{1/3} = 4.1$ times as much calcite, about 50 mg/L in open air and 200 mg/L in the soil.'],
      a: 'Soil water has a pH of 4.7 against 5.6, and carries about four times as much limestone: the work is done underground.' },
    { title: 'A karst spring', q: 'A spring that drains a limestone plateau flows at 0.2 m³/s with 250 mg/L of dissolved CaCO₃. How much rock does the spring remove in a year, and how much CO₂ does that take from the air?',
      steps: ['Mass per year: $0.2\\ \\mathrm{m^3/s} \\times 0.25\\ \\mathrm{kg/m^3} \\times 3.156\\times10^{7}\\ \\mathrm{s} = 1.6\\times10^{6}$ kg, or 1,580 t.', 'Volume of rock at 2,700 kg/m³: $1.58\\times10^{6}/2700 \\approx 580\\ \\mathrm{m^3}$ a year.', 'CO₂ consumed: $1580 \\times 44.01/100.09 \\approx 695$ t.'],
      a: 'The spring removes about 1,600 t of limestone (580 m³) and about 700 t of CO₂ every year — a cavity the size of a house every few years, somewhere underground.' }
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'Why is rain in perfectly clean air slightly acidic?', choices: ['It picks up sulfur from the sea', 'It dissolves CO₂, forming carbonic acid', 'The hydrogen bonds donate protons', 'Lightning makes nitric acid'], a: 1, why: 'Carbon dioxide dissolves and partly reacts with water. At 420 ppm that gives a pH of about 5.6; the sulfur and nitrogen oxides of pollution push it lower.' },
    { q: 'Dissolving limestone releases CO₂ into the air.', a: false, why: 'CaCO₃ + CO₂ + H₂O → Ca²⁺ + 2HCO₃⁻ consumes CO₂. The release happens in a cave when a drop loses CO₂ and calcite is deposited, or later when the ocean precipitates carbonate.' },
    { q: 'Water that has been through soil dissolves much more limestone than water straight from the sky because', choices: ['the soil adds salt', 'roots and microbes raise the CO₂ in the soil air', 'the soil is warmer', 'the soil water is purer'], a: 1, why: 'Respiration enriches soil air to 1–5 % CO₂, ten to a hundred times the open air, which makes the water more acid and raises the calcite it can carry.' },
    { q: 'A stalagmite 3 m tall has been growing at 0.1 mm per year. How old is it, in thousand years?', answer: 30, why: '3 m = 3 000 mm; 3 000 mm / 0.1 mm per year = 30 000 years.' },
    { q: 'A warming climate speeds up chemical weathering. What is the effect on atmospheric CO₂?', choices: ['It rises, because weathering releases carbon', 'It falls, because weathering consumes CO₂', 'It does not change, because weathering is too slow to matter', 'It rises, then falls, within a year'], a: 1, why: 'Faster weathering takes more CO₂ from the air, weakening the greenhouse and cooling the planet. It is a negative feedback, but a slow one: hundreds of thousands of years.' }
  ],
  applications: ['Water supply: about a quarter of the world\'s people drink from karst aquifers, which are fast and poorly filtered, so they are easily polluted.', 'Climate records: a stalagmite is dated by uranium–thorium to within a few thousand years, and its oxygen isotopes record the rain that fed it for hundreds of thousands of years.', 'Buildings and statues: limestone and marble dissolve in rain, and faster in acid rain.', 'Carbon removal: spreading finely crushed basalt on fields uses the same reaction to lock up CO₂ as bicarbonate.', 'Engineering geology: sinkholes and cave roofs are a hazard under roads, quarries and dam sites on limestone.'],
  history: 'The word karst is from the Slovene Kras. Urey suggested in 1952 that the weathering of silicate rock controls the CO₂ of the air; Walker, Hays and Kasting\'s 1981 paper put in the temperature dependence that makes it a thermostat.',
  sources: ['Press and Siever / Grotzinger and Jordan, *Understanding Earth*, chapter on weathering and soils.', 'Marshak, *Earth: Portrait of a Planet*, chapter on weathering.', 'Walker, J. C. G., Hays, P. B. and Kasting, J. F. (1981), A negative feedback mechanism for the long-term stabilization of Earth\'s surface temperature, *Journal of Geophysical Research* 86, 9776–9782.', 'White, W. B. (1988), *Geomorphology and Hydrology of Karst Terrains*, Oxford University Press.'],
  sim: 'geo-limestone'
},

/* ================================================================ glaciers sculpt */
{
  id: 'glaciers-sculpt', parent: 'what-the-water-has-been-doing', title: 'Glaciers sculpt', level: 2,
  short: 'Ice a kilometre thick grinds and plucks the rock beneath it. A glacier turns a river\'s V-shaped valley into a U, carves fjords deeper than the sea outside, leaves the Great Lakes behind — and its weight presses the crust down by hundreds of metres, which is still springing back.',
  keywords: ['glacier', 'abrasion', 'plucking', 'U-shaped valley', 'fjord', 'cirque', 'moraine', 'erratic', 'till', 'Great Lakes', 'isostatic rebound', 'Scandinavia', 'Sognefjord', 'Yosemite', 'trimline'],
  prereq: ['glaciers-flow', 'why-ice-floats', 'rivers-carve'],
  related: ['frost-shatters-rock', 'icebergs-and-sea-ice', 'the-deep-time-of-water', 'frost-wedging-and-burst-pipes', 'high-pressure-ices', 'physics:buoyancy', 'physics:planets'],
  body: `
A glacier is a river of ice. It is a slow one — metres a day at best, usually centimetres — but it can be a kilometre thick, it is heavy, and it carries tools. It wears the bed by two mechanisms. **Abrasion**: rock fragments frozen into the base of the ice scour the bed like sandpaper, leaving scratches (striations) and a fine flour of ground rock that makes glacial meltwater milky. **Plucking**: meltwater seeps into joints of the bedrock, freezes onto them, and as the ice moves on it tears whole blocks out.

### Why ice slides
At the bottom of a thick glacier the pressure is high — 18 MPa under 2 km of ice. Ice is less dense than water ([[why-ice-floats]]), so squeezing it favours the liquid and *lowers* its melting point, by 0.0074 K per bar: the bed of a glacier a kilometre thick is wet at about −0.7 °C. A thin film of meltwater lubricates the flow. Where the bed is frozen to the rock the ice does not slide and does little; where it is wet it erodes at millimetres a year.

### From V to U
A river cuts a V. A glacier fills the valley and erodes the bed everywhere it touches it, walls as well as floor, at a roughly equal rate. Moving a V-shaped boundary an equal distance everywhere rounds off its bottom into a circle, so the valley becomes a **U**: a flat or rounded floor, steep walls, and above the ice surface the untouched rock of the old V, with a sharp edge, the trimline, between them. Side streams are left hanging above the new floor: Yosemite Falls, 740 m high, pours from a hanging valley. Where the valley floor drops below sea level, the sea floods in and it is a **fjord**. Sognefjord in Norway is 204 km long and 1,300 m deep, much deeper than the sea at its mouth. An ice stream that long and that heavy can only have cut so deep while it was grounded: ice floats once its thickness is less than 1.12 times the water depth, so to cut 1,300 m the ice had to be nearly 1,450 m thick.

### What is left behind
When a glacier melts it dumps what it carried. **Till** is the unsorted mixture of clay to boulder; a **moraine** is a ridge of it at the side or the end of the ice; an **erratic** is a boulder carried hundreds of kilometres from its source and left on different rock, which once puzzled people into inventing a flood. The Great Lakes were gouged out of river valleys and lowlands by the Laurentide ice sheet, and now hold about a fifth of the world's surface fresh water (Superior alone is 406 m deep).

### The land that rises
Two kilometres of ice have a weight the crust cannot bear: it floats on the mantle, so it sinks until the displaced mantle weighs the same as the ice. The depression is $d = H\\rho_{ice}/\\rho_{mantle}$, about 28 % of the ice thickness: 700 m under 2.5 km of ice. When the ice melted about 10,000 years ago the mantle began to flow back and the crust to rise. The head of the Gulf of Bothnia still rises at about 9 mm a year, the old shorelines of the Baltic stand nearly 300 m above the sea, and Finland gains several square kilometres of land each year. The rate of the rebound measures the viscosity of the mantle: about $10^{21}$ Pa·s, a hundred billion billion times that of honey.

> [!key] A glacier erodes its bed by abrasion and plucking, turns V-valleys into U-valleys, fjords and cirques, and leaves moraines and erratics. The weight of the ice sinks the crust by a quarter of its thickness, and the land is still rising by millimetres a year.`,
  ideas: ['Ice erodes by abrasion (rock fragments in the ice) and plucking (blocks torn out of frozen-on joints); it is most effective where the base is at the melting point.', 'Erosion roughly equal at all points of the bed rounds the V of a river valley into a U; above the ice, the old V remains, with a trimline.', 'A glacier cuts below sea level only while grounded, which needs ice about 1.12 times as thick as the water is deep.', 'Ice sinks the crust by about 28 % of its thickness; the mantle flows back slowly after the ice has gone.'],
  pitfalls: ['"Glaciers carve valleys from scratch" — Mostly they reshape valleys that rivers cut first, widening and deepening them; the V becomes a U.', '"The land rises after the ice melts because it is lighter" — Rebound is slow because the mantle is viscous: the land is still rising 10,000 years after the ice has gone.', '"Erratic boulders were pushed by floods" — They were carried inside or on the ice, which is the only agent that can move a house-size boulder hundreds of kilometres and leave it on different rock.'],
  terms: [
    { term: 'Abrasion and plucking', also: ['glacial erosion', 'quarrying'], def: 'The two ways ice wears its bed: abrasion is the scouring of the rock by fragments held in the ice, and plucking is the tearing out of blocks from jointed rock when meltwater freezes onto them.' },
    { term: 'Glacial trough', also: ['U-shaped valley', 'glacial valley'], def: 'A valley with a rounded or flat floor and steep walls, made by a glacier widening and deepening a river valley; hanging valleys and waterfalls lie on its walls. A fjord is a trough below sea level.' },
    { term: 'Fjord', also: ['fiord', 'glacial inlet'], def: 'A long, narrow arm of the sea in a glacial trough, often deeper than the sea outside because of a rock sill at its mouth. Sognefjord, Norway: 204 km long, 1,300 m deep.' },
    { term: 'Moraine', also: ['till', 'terminal moraine', 'lateral moraine'], def: 'A ridge or sheet of till — the unsorted debris of clay, sand, gravel and boulders — laid down at the edge of a glacier or beneath it.' },
    { term: 'Erratic', also: ['glacial erratic'], def: 'A boulder carried by ice and left on rock of a different type, sometimes hundreds of kilometres from where it came from.' },
    { term: 'Isostatic rebound', also: ['glacial isostatic adjustment', 'post-glacial rebound', 'GIA'], def: 'The slow rise of the land after the weight of the ice has been removed, as the mantle flows back under it. Scandinavia rises by up to 9 mm a year, with a relaxation time of several thousand years.' }
  ],
  formulas: [
    { name: 'Depression of the crust under an ice sheet', expr: 'd = H*rhoI/rhoM', tex: 'd = H\\,\\frac{\\rho_{ice}}{\\rho_{m}}',
      vars: { d: { name: 'depression of the land', q: 'length', unit: 'm' }, H: { name: 'thickness of the ice', q: 'length', unit: 'm', value: 2500 }, rhoI: { name: 'density of ice', q: 'density', unit: 'kg/m³', value: 917, tex: '\\rho_{ice}' }, rhoM: { name: 'density of the mantle', q: 'density', unit: 'kg/m³', value: 3300, tex: '\\rho_{m}', hint: 'About 3,300 kg/m³ for the upper mantle.' } },
      solveFor: 'd', note: 'For full local isostatic equilibrium: the displaced mantle weighs as much as the ice. Reached only after thousands of years, and the real response is spread over a wider area than the load.',
      stories: { d: 'A sheet of ice {H} thick lies on the crust. By how much does the land sink once it has come into balance?', H: 'The land under an ice sheet has sunk by {d}. How thick is the ice?' } },
    { name: 'Rate of rebound', expr: 'v = u/tau', tex: 'v = \\frac{u}{\\tau}',
      vars: { v: { name: 'rate of rise', q: false, unit: 'mm/yr' }, u: { name: 'uplift still to come', q: false, unit: 'm', value: 90 }, tau: { name: 'relaxation time of the mantle', q: false, unit: 'kyr', value: 10, hint: 'Several thousand years in the simplest model, longer in models with a stiff lower mantle.' } },
      solveFor: 'v', note: 'For an exponential approach to equilibrium, $u(t) = u_0 e^{-t/\\tau}$, the rate is the remaining uplift divided by the relaxation time. Metres divided by thousands of years is millimetres a year.',
      stories: { v: 'The land has {u} left to rise, with a relaxation time of {tau}. How fast does it rise now?', u: 'The land rises at {v} with a relaxation time of {tau}. How much uplift is still to come?' } },
    { name: 'Ice thickness needed to cut a trough below sea level', expr: 'H = D*rhoW/rhoI', tex: 'H = D\\,\\frac{\\rho_w}{\\rho_{ice}}',
      vars: { H: { name: 'ice thickness needed', q: 'length', unit: 'm' }, D: { name: 'depth of the trough below sea level', q: 'length', unit: 'm', value: 1300 }, rhoW: { name: 'density of sea water', q: 'density', unit: 'kg/m³', value: 1025, tex: '\\rho_w' }, rhoI: { name: 'density of ice', q: 'density', unit: 'kg/m³', value: 917, tex: '\\rho_{ice}' } },
      solveFor: 'H', note: 'A glacier stays grounded, and so keeps cutting, only while it is too heavy to float: its thickness must exceed this.',
      stories: { H: 'A fjord is {D} deep. How thick must the ice have been to cut it while grounded?' } },
    { name: 'Pressure under the ice', expr: 'p = rhoI*g*H', tex: 'p = \\rho_{ice}\\, g\\, H',
      vars: { p: { name: 'pressure at the bed', q: 'pressure', unit: 'MPa' }, rhoI: { name: 'density of ice', q: 'density', unit: 'kg/m³', value: 917, tex: '\\rho_{ice}' }, g: { const: 'g' }, H: { name: 'thickness of the ice', q: 'length', unit: 'm', value: 2000 } },
      solveFor: 'p', note: 'The melting point falls by 0.0074 K for every bar: 18 MPa is 180 bar and lowers it by 1.3 K.',
      stories: { p: 'The ice is {H} thick. What is the pressure at its base?' } }
  ],
  examples: [
    { title: 'How far did Scandinavia sink?', q: 'The Fennoscandian ice sheet was about 2.5 km thick at its centre. By how much did it depress the land, and if the land has been rising with a relaxation time of 3.5 thousand years for 10 thousand years, how much remains and how fast is it rising?',
      steps: [{ text: 'Depression:', tex: 'd = 2500 \\times \\frac{917}{3300} = 695\\ \\mathrm{m}' }, { text: 'Still to rise after 10 kyr:', tex: 'u = 695\\,e^{-10/3.5} = 40\\ \\mathrm{m}' }, { text: 'Rate now:', tex: 'v = \\frac{u}{\\tau} = \\frac{40\\ \\mathrm{m}}{3.5\\ \\mathrm{kyr}} = 11\\ \\mathrm{mm/yr}' }],
      a: 'The land sank about 700 m, 40 m of which is still to come, at about 11 mm a year — the right order of magnitude (the Gulf of Bothnia is observed to rise by up to 9 mm a year). The real mantle has layers of different viscosity, which is why a single relaxation time gives only an approximate fit.' },
    { title: 'Cutting a fjord', q: 'A fjord is 1,300 m deep. How thick did the glacier have to be, and what pressure was there under it?',
      steps: [{ text: 'To stay grounded:', tex: 'H = 1300 \\times \\frac{1025}{917} = 1453\\ \\mathrm{m}' }, { text: 'Pressure at the bed of that ice:', tex: 'p = 917 \\times 9.81 \\times 1453 = 13\\ \\mathrm{MPa}' }, 'The ice floats if it is thinner, and a floating glacier does not erode its bed.'],
      a: 'About 1,450 m of ice, putting 13 MPa on the bed: a glacier of that thickness, flowing for tens of thousands of years, is what a deep fjord needs.' }
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'Why does a glacier turn a V-shaped valley into a U?', choices: ['It melts the walls', 'It wears the bed everywhere it touches, so the bottom of the V is rounded and the walls move outwards', 'Rivers always flow into glaciers', 'Ice is soft and flows into the shape of a U'], a: 1, why: 'Equal erosion along all of a V-shaped boundary rounds the V\'s bottom into a circular arc: that is the U. Above the ice surface the old V remains.' },
    { q: 'A fjord can be deeper than the sea at its mouth.', a: true, why: 'The glacier cut the trough while it was too heavy to float; the sea flooded it after the ice melted. A rock sill at the mouth, where the ice was thinner, is typical.' },
    { q: 'How far does a 3 km ice sheet depress the crust once it is in balance? (mantle density 3,300 kg/m³)', answer: 834, unit: 'm', why: 'd = 3000 × 917/3300 = 834 m.' },
    { q: 'The land under Scandinavia is still rising ten thousand years after the ice went. The reason is', choices: ['The ice is still melting', 'The mantle is viscous and flows back slowly', 'The sea is falling', 'The crust is warming up'], a: 1, why: 'The crust floats on the mantle, which must flow horizontally back under the land; with a viscosity of about 10²¹ Pa·s that takes thousands of years.' },
    { q: 'The base of a thick glacier is at the melting point because', choices: ['the pressure lowers the melting point of ice', 'friction with the rock is always enough to melt it', 'the air above is warm', 'ice cannot be colder than 0 °C'], a: 0, why: 'Ice is less dense than water, so pressure favours the liquid and the melting point drops by 0.0074 K per bar. Friction and heat from the ground help.' }
  ],
  applications: ['Landscape and water: the Great Lakes, the lakes of Finland and Canada, and the fjords of Norway, Chile and New Zealand are all glacial; moraine ridges dam lakes and hold aquifers.', 'Sand and gravel for roads and concrete are mostly glacial outwash.', 'Sea-level science: tide gauges and GPS must be corrected for rebound before the rise of the sea is read from them.', 'The mantle\'s viscosity, a quantity no one can measure directly, comes from the rate of rebound; Haskell used Scandinavia in 1935.', 'Finland and Sweden are slowly gaining land: harbours silt up and old ports are left inland.'],
  history: 'Louis Agassiz\'s 1837 address in Neuchâtel proposed a great ice age and ended the flood explanation of erratics; Jamieson suggested in 1865 that the weight of ice had depressed Scotland; Haskell\'s 1935 paper turned Scandinavia\'s uplift into a viscosity of the mantle.',
  sources: ['Press and Siever / Grotzinger and Jordan, *Understanding Earth*, chapter on glaciers.', 'Benn, D. I. and Evans, D. J. A., *Glaciers and Glaciation* (2nd ed.), Hodder.', 'Harbor, J. M. (1992), Numerical modeling of the development of U-shaped valleys by glacial erosion, *Geological Society of America Bulletin* 104, 1364–1375.', 'Peltier, W. R. (2004), Global glacial isostasy and the surface of the ice-age Earth: the ICE-5G (VM2) model, *Annual Review of Earth and Planetary Sciences* 32, 111–149.'],
  sim: 'geo-glacier'
}
